/**
  @page examples-geometry-connectivity Computing mesh connectivity
  @brief Inspect edge-to-cell incidence on a square split into two triangles.

  Coordinates locate vertices in space. Connectivity records incidence between
  vertices, edges, and cells. A mesh of two triangles is used to enumerate these
  relations explicitly.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-geom-conn-compute Construct the incidence relations

  A two-by-two vertex grid gives four vertices and two triangles. The diagonal
  is an interior edge shared by both cells; the four exterior edges belong to
  one cell each.

  @snippet Geometry/Connectivity.cpp connectivity-mesh

  `compute(1, 2)` constructs edge-to-cell incidence. `compute(2, 1)` constructs
  the reverse cell-to-edge relation. These are requested explicitly before
  queries that depend on them.

  @section examples-geom-conn-visualization Inspect the actual topology

  @image html Geometry/Connectivity.png "The generated square mesh. Exterior edges have one incident cell; the interior diagonal has two. Labels use the indices exported by the C++ program." width=560px

  The diagram uses the vertex and edge indices exported by the program.
  Four vertices, five edges, and two cells give the Euler count
  @f$4-5+2=1@f$ for this triangulated disk.

  @section examples-geom-conn-boundary Query the incident cells

  @snippet Geometry/Connectivity.cpp connectivity-incidence

  On a conforming two-dimensional manifold mesh, an exterior edge has one incident
  cell and an interior edge has two. For non-manifold input, incidence counts must
  be interpreted using the mesh topology.

  This edge-to-cell relation is also what lets the
  @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" recognize exterior edges for
  its Dirichlet condition. Boundary labels and boundary detection are separate:
  an integer attribute selects a region after the topology identifies it.

  @section examples-geom-conn-common Select the relation needed by the operation

  | Relation on this 2D mesh | Question answered |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `compute(1, 2)` | Which cells contain an edge? |
  | `compute(2, 1)` | Which edges bound a cell? |
  | `compute(0, 2)` | Which cells contain a vertex? |

  Incidence records shared entities. Geometric proximity is determined from
  coordinates, while same-dimension adjacency follows the relation documented by
  its API.

  @section examples-geom-conn-full Complete program

  Build the `Connectivity` example target and run it from an output directory.
  It prints the incident-cell counts and writes `Connectivity.vertices.csv`
  and `Connectivity.edges.csv`, which reproduce the diagram.

  @snippet Geometry/Connectivity.cpp connectivity-source

  See @ref Geometry/Connectivity.cpp "Connectivity.cpp", the
  @ref guides-connectivity "connectivity guide", and the
  @ref guides-polytopes "polytope guide" for related concepts.

  @example Geometry/Connectivity.cpp
  @m_examplenavigation{examples-geometry-connectivity,Geometry/}
  @m_footernavigation
*/
